WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.622
Suppose a public archive receives one thousand page
requests per second.

00:00:04.622 --> 00:00:10.078
If a shared cache answers eighty percent, two
hundred requests reach the application.

00:00:10.078 --> 00:00:16.369
If each remaining page needs three database reads,
the uncached read workload is approximately six

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:17.974
hundred reads per second.

00:00:17.974 --> 00:00:24.393
These are deliberately simplified assumptions, but
they show why cache effectiveness must be applied

00:00:24.393 --> 00:00:26.126
before sizing the database.

00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:32.340
Adding application workers changes application
capacity; it does not by itself change those six

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:33.256
hundred reads.

00:00:33.256 --> 00:00:38.031
Classify the remaining requests before distributing
them across replicas.

00:00:38.031 --> 00:00:44.114
A visitor reading an old exhibit may tolerate a
short delay, while a curator checking an edit

00:00:44.114 --> 00:00:47.188
immediately after saving expects the new state.

00:00:47.226 --> 00:00:53.481
The first path can use a lagging replica if the
product permits it; the second may require a primary

00:00:53.481 --> 00:00:56.420
read or another explicit consistency mechanism.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.548
A scalable diagram therefore needs routing rules as
well as boxes.

00:01:00.548 --> 00:01:06.677
Measure the hit rate and query count under a
representative mix rather than assuming every
request

00:01:06.677 --> 00:01:07.740
benefits equally.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:13.160
An illustrative service records twenty-six million
updates over a thirty-day month.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:18.775
Dividing by approximately 2.6 million seconds gives
ten updates per second on average.

00:01:18.775 --> 00:01:23.607
That calculation is useful for checking magnitude,
but it conceals timing.

00:01:23.607 --> 00:01:30.136
If a news event produces one hundred updates per
second for several minutes, a system sized only for

00:01:30.136 --> 00:01:36.535
the average can accumulate a backlog or reject
requests despite apparently modest monthly traffic.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.964
Also expand the meaning of an update.

00:01:38.964 --> 00:01:44.924
One user action may write a primary row, update
indexes and append an audit record inside a

00:01:44.924 --> 00:01:45.710
transaction.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:52.063
Those actions do not map to a fixed number of
physical disk operations because buffering, logging

00:01:52.063 --> 00:01:54.159
and storage behaviour intervene.

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:59.465
Test the complete transaction with realistic data,
contention and burst duration.

00:01:59.465 --> 00:02:02.685
Report latency and error rate alongside throughput.

00:02:02.685 --> 00:02:08.809
The decision to retain one primary should follow
those measurements and consistency needs, rather

00:02:08.809 --> 00:02:14.680
than a direct comparison between user events and a
device's advertised operations per second.
